#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Build the gated index.html from upstream's, by SUBTRACTION.

    hdw4s-gate-index <upstream index.html> <output index.html>
    HDW4S_SESSION_NAME names the session on the card and in the tab.
    HDW4S_GATE_MODE   takeover (default) | mint | off -- what arrival does.
    HDW4S_SHARE_MODE  off (default) | view | control  -- what sharing offers.
    HDW4S_DIRECTORY   no (default) | yes -- does the door this page is served
                      through ALSO serve /sessions/, the session directory?

The gate and share modes are DEFAULTS, not settings. Each is also readable from the query string
("?gate=off", "?share=view"), and the query string wins. That is deliberate and it is
the cheaper carrier of the two: changing a baked default means re-running
"hdw4s-webroot build", which copies a fresh tree from the packaged client and therefore
removes the live tree's "hdw4s-incarnation" file until the next session start
republishes it. A URL costs nothing and lets two arms be open side by side, which is
what comparing them requires.

The two knobs were built to settle two questions by use rather than by argument --
whether arrival should ask before handing over a desktop, and what a sharing link says
it grants -- and this paragraph used to end "remove them once each is settled".

THE FIRST IS SETTLED AND THE ANSWER WAS "KEEP IT". Ruled 2026-09-22: gate wherever
automatic action could do damage, and stealing a session is damage. That makes the gate
a permanent per-session-type setting rather than scaffolding -- ask on a fresh tab for
both session kinds, at a cost of one click, accepted knowingly. So DO NOT delete the
gate arm as tidying-up: the instruction to do so outlived its question, and following it
would remove a control the owner asked to keep.

The sharing knob is still open and the original reasoning still applies to it: a knob
kept past its decision is a decision nobody made.

Upstream's document is taken as-is; only the module <script> is removed -- that removal
IS the gate, because both transports evict on connect and a client that never loads
never connects -- and an overlay is appended. Nothing else upstream ships is touched.

Not a fork and not a monkey patch: upstream publishes this as a host-page seam, and the
re-entry the card uses, window.selkiesCoreInitialize(), is its documented entry point.

Every behaviour here was put in by a test that caught it missing; the comments say which,
because each one looks like an unnecessary special case until it is not.

HDW4S_DIRECTORY IS NOT A KNOB, AND UNLIKE THE TWO ABOVE IT IS NOT READABLE FROM THE
QUERY STRING. It is not a choice about what to offer; it is a fact about the deployment
that the builder knows and the page cannot discover, so there is no second arm to put
side by side and nothing to settle by use. A "?directory=yes" would simply let anyone
put the defect below back on any page.

THE DEFECT, measured 2026-09-26 on a development box through a named session's own
front door: the card's "Your desktops" line was in this overlay unconditionally, so
it shipped on
NAMED desktops too -- and /sessions/ is a route of hdw4s-demux, which is the EPHEMERAL
front door. A named desktop is reached reverse proxy -> hdw4s-proxy@<inst>.socket ->
systemd-socket-proxyd -> Selkies' own aiohttp server, and NOTHING OF OURS IS IN THAT
PATH. So the link was not pointing at the wrong place; there is no component on that
origin that could answer it at all, and no spelling of the href would have been better.
GET /sessions/ returned "404 Not Found", zero-length body, "Server: Python/3.12
aiohttp/3.14.3" -- the owner pressed a line on our card and was handed a blank page.
The positive control in the same run: GET / on the same door returned 200, 43706 bytes,
carrying the gate marker and the words "Your desktops", so the empty answer was about
that path and not about a rig that could not reach the box.

It therefore defaults to NO, and the default is the whole safety property: a card that
stays quiet is never wrong, and a card that offers a page its own door cannot serve is
wrong in the expensive direction -- the one line on the card that is not the button is
the line a person reaches for when the desktop in front of them has stopped working.

WHAT IS NOT HERE, so that the next reader does not conclude it was overlooked: a named
desktop still has no way for its occupant to end or restart it from the browser. That
is the thing the owner actually asked for, it cannot be served from this page for the
reason above, and the options are written down in
private/NAMED-SESSION-CONTROL-BRIEF.md rather than chosen here.
"""
import json, os, re, sys
from html import escape as html_escape
src, dst = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2]
html = open(src, encoding="utf-8").read()
mods = re.findall(r'<script\b[^>]*type=["\']module["\'][^>]*></script>', html)
if len(mods) != 1:
    raise SystemExit("expected exactly one module script, found %d -- refusing to guess"
                     % len(mods))
src_attr = re.search(r'src=["\']([^"\']+)["\']', mods[0]).group(1)
html = html.replace(mods[0], "<!-- hdw4s: module deferred behind the boot gate -->")

OVERLAY = r"""
<style>
 /* The [hidden] rule MUST come with the display rule and MUST win. An author
