<script>
(() => {
  var SRC = __SRC__;
  var KEY = 'hdw4s_booted_' + location.pathname;
  var EXPECT = KEY + ':expect-reload';
  var INC = KEY + ':incarnation';
  // WHETHER ANYONE WAS LOOKING AT THIS TAB WHEN IT LOADED, read at the first
  // statement of the overlay and never again: by the time the arrival decision
  // is taken the answer may have changed, and the question is about the load.
  //
  // This is the whole discriminator the repair below rests on, so it is worth
  // saying why it is one. A person pressing F5 is LOOKING at the tab, so their
  // load is visible. The streaming client's own recovery reload happens while
  // the tab is in the background, because that is the only state that produces
  // it: the client sends STOP_VIDEO when the document hides, goes quiet, the
  // server's thirty-second heartbeat finds the socket gone, and the client
  // reloads the page to reconnect. Measured 2026-09-25 on the owner's own
  // session: STOP_VIDEO at 11:36:41, disconnect at 11:37:11, three times in
  // five minutes, thirty seconds apart to the second each time.
  var LOADED_HIDDEN = (document.visibilityState === 'hidden');
  var gate = document.getElementById('hdw4s-gate');
  var body = document.getElementById('hdw4s-body');
  var go   = document.getElementById('hdw4s-go');
  var booted = false;

  // --- the two knobs, and why a bad one is loud -------------------------------------
  // CFG is baked at generation; the query string wins over it. A value that is not one
  // of the arms does NOT quietly fall back: it falls back AND says so, on the screen.
  // An instrument that ignores its own knob reports on an arm nobody chose, and this
  // project has already shipped a QA matrix that tested one transport twice because a
  // mis-set knob was silently ignored.
  var CFG = __CFG__;
  var BADKNOB = [];
  function pick(name, allowed){
    var v = null;
    try { v = new URLSearchParams(location.search).get(name); } catch(e){}
    if (v === null || v === '') return CFG[name];
    if (allowed.indexOf(v) >= 0) return v;
    BADKNOB.push(name + '=' + v + ' (not one of ' + allowed.join('/') + ')');
    return CFG[name];
  }
  var GATE  = pick('gate',  ['takeover','mint','off']);
  var SHARE = pick('share', ['off','view','control']);

  // WHAT AN EVICTED PAGE ACTUALLY DOES, measured on both transports at upstream
  // selkies-project/selkies 2.0.0rc0-188-g8fd2aaef. This differs sharply between the
  // two, at exactly the moment this overlay depends on, so nothing here may assume one.
  //
  // NOT RE-MEASURED, AND NO LONGER RE-MEASURABLE AT THAT BUILD. It was a branch tip
  // upstream has since squashed and force-pushed, so that revision exists nowhere any
  // more. What the updater now installs is 2.0.0rc1, which renamed webrtc_mode.py and
  // websockets_mode.py and moved selkies-ws-core.js -- exactly the files this paragraph
  // is about. Nobody has watched a page get evicted on rc1. Read the two behaviours
  // below as the reason this code has the shape it has, not as a statement about the
  // build in front of you; measure them again before relying on either.
  //
  //   websockets  the last frame is RETAINED. The video element is still present,
  //               srcObject still set, same dimensions, and the picture survives with
  //               and without upstream's status bar, which is drawn on top of it.
  //   WebRTC      upstream raises a NATIVE alert() on this ordinary lifecycle event
  //               ("Disconnected: ... Please try again."). A native modal blocks the
  //               page's main thread, so nothing in the page can respond -- including
  //               this wrapper -- until a person clicks OK. After dismissal the page is
  //               completely alive, and upstream's own status then reads "Connected"
  //               for a session it has just been evicted from.
  //
  // RETRACTION, kept because it cost an afternoon and the wrong answer is the reachable
  // one: that unresponsive WebRTC tab was diagnosed as WEDGED, by three separate probes
  // that each ruled something out and were each correct in what they measured. They all
  // asked the page, and the page was behind a modal. There is no wedge. The screenshot
  // was captured at the start and not looked at. Count nothing you have not looked at.
  //
  // AND THE TRANSPORT IS NOT A PROPERTY OF THE SESSION TYPE. It is whatever the
  // administrator configured; either choice is legitimate. So "named sessions behave
  // this way and ephemeral ones that way" is false -- both behaviours are reachable for
  // any session. This overlay must therefore work WITHOUT depending on either
  // mechanism, which is why it is markup placed behind the video at page load rather
  // than anything that has to run at the moment of eviction. That is a requirement, not
  // a convenience: on WebRTC the modal appears before our code gets a turn.
  //
  // Two states, kept apart on purpose. Cold: this tab has never connected and cannot
  // know what is open elsewhere, so the consequence is conditional. Lost: this tab WAS
  // connected and was disconnected, which is a fact and is stated as one.
  //
  // THE CONDITIONAL IS NOT CAUTION, IT IS THE ONLY HONEST FORM, and that is now
  // measured rather than reasoned. Every signal this product has for "somebody is
  // connected" was falsified twice in one day: the occupancy probe asked a port an
  // ephemeral slot never uses and answered zero for every session under every
  // condition, and a real visitor's session read as having nobody on it for the first
  // twenty-four minutes of its life while being the freshest session on the box. So
  // nothing here may assert who else is connected -- not because we are being careful,
  // but because we would be wrong.
  //
  // AND THE ORDER OF THE SENTENCES IS THE REPAIR. This used to open by telling the
  // reader that connecting "will take it over", which is written for a stranger
  // arriving at somebody else's desktop. On the commonest path by far it is the same
  // person's own tab coming back -- returning from a backgrounded tab, or after the
  // stream dropped -- and that reader was being warned they were about to steal from
  // themselves. The consequence is still stated in full, because it is real and the
  // person may indeed have another tab open; it is stated SECOND, after the case that
  // is usually theirs, and without the vocabulary of theft. Found by opening the
  // picture rather than by reading a verdict.
  var COLD = '<p>This tab is not connected.</p>'
    + '<p>Usually that just means it is a new tab, or one that has been sitting idle. '
    + 'Connecting brings the desktop back here as it was.</p>'
    + '<p>If you also have this desktop open in another tab or on another device, that '
    + 'one will stop streaming and keep showing its last picture until you close it. '
    + 'Nothing in the desktop is lost either way.</p>';
  var LOST = '<p>This tab was disconnected: the desktop was opened somewhere else.</p>'
    + '<p>Nothing has been lost. The desktop is still running, and you can bring it '
    + 'back here.</p>';

  // The button's words are part of the state, not decoration around it. It used to
  // read "Take over and disconnect other sessions" in static markup, on every state
  // including COLD -- where the comment four lines above says this tab CANNOT know
  // what is open elsewhere. So the button asserted as fact the one thing the body
  // was careful to state as a condition, and it did so on the common path.
  //
  // Each label reuses the verb its own body text just used: COLD says "connecting
  // here", LOST says "bring it back here". That is what makes this screen and the
  // session directory siblings -- shared vocabulary, not a shared palette.
  var COLD_GO = 'Connect';
  var LOST_GO = 'Bring it back here';

  // The THIRD arrival arm, and the reason there are three rather than two. The
  // committed shape for this product is that arriving always mints a fresh desktop
  // and never resumes one -- at which point COLD's whole body, a warning about taking
  // a desktop away from another tab, is a warning about something that will not
  // happen. Offering only "COLD or nothing" would ask him to rule on a card the design
  // has already retired. This arm is what arrival says once R1 holds; it is text, it
  // costs nothing, and without it the comparison is between the wrong two things.
  //
  // WHAT THIS CARD SAYS ABOUT LIFETIME IS THE PRODUCT'S ONLY STATEMENT ABOUT IT, and
  // it was false in the dangerous direction until 2026-09-23. It read "nothing in it
  // survives being closed". Closing the tab does not stop the desktop: the session is
  // held alive and is reclaimed later, after a period with nobody connected, so a
  // person who signed into webmail and closed the tab believing it was gone was still
  // signed in. A promise that something is destroyed is the one kind of promise where
  // being wrong costs the reader rather than us, so it is now stated the other way
  // round -- the desktop OUTLIVES the tab -- and the sign-out is named as the act that
  // actually ends the exposure, because it is the only one the person can perform.
  //
  // NO NUMBER APPEARS HERE, DELIBERATELY. The reclaim period is a per-instance setting
  // an administrator changes with "set", the page is generated once per web-root build,
  // and a rebuild is not triggered by changing the setting. A figure baked in here
  // would therefore be a claim that goes stale silently and breaks nothing -- and it
  // would be wrong for every instance that overrides the default, since one built tree
  // is served for the name it was built for. "for a while" is weaker and stays true.
  // If a number is ever wanted on this card it has to arrive at page load from the
  // session that is actually serving it, not from generation time.
  //
  // THE LAST LINE IS NOT DECORATION. Reachability is per browser per device, so a
  // person who starts a desktop on a phone and opens the same address on a laptop gets
  // a different one. Unexplained that is a fault report; stated once in advance it is
  // a rule, and rules are forgivable.
  //
  // NOTHING HERE TELLS THE PERSON TO CLOSE THE DESKTOP THEMSELVES, and the reason
  // CHANGED on 2026-09-25 rather than going away. It used to be that no visitor-facing
  // control ended a session at all, so the instruction would have been a documented
  // act that cannot be performed. There is one now -- Discard, in the session
  // directory -- so the reason is no longer "it is impossible": it is that this card
  // is read by somebody deciding whether to CONNECT, and a destructive verb does not
  // belong beside that decision. The directory is one quiet link away, under the
  // button, and that link is where ending one lives.
  //
  // THE LINK IS ALSO THE OWNER'S RULING OF 2026-09-25 reaching this card: *"instead
  // of just offering me to take back the session, this should also be the place where
  // i can create a brand new one."* It is a LINK and not a second button, because the
  // card's whole mechanism is that one object looks pressable -- and because the
  // thing it leads to costs a slot, so it is reached by a page a person reads rather
  // than by a control beside the one they came to press. The directory's own create
  // is a POST, so following this link mints nothing.
  var MINT = '<p>This will start a fresh desktop. Nothing from anyone else is in it.</p>'
    + '<p><b>Closing this tab does not end it.</b> It keeps running, still signed in to '
    + 'whatever you sign in to, and you can come back to it in this browser. Sign out of '
    + 'anything that matters before you walk away.</p>'
    + '<p>It is not kept: once nobody has connected to it for a while it ends by itself, '
    + 'and everything in it is destroyed then. Nothing in it is backed up.</p>'
    + '<p>This browser on this device is what reaches it. Another browser, or another '
    + 'device, gets a different desktop.</p>';
  var MINT_GO = 'Start a fresh desktop';

  // THE ENDED PAGE, reached by asking the router for it. A query string makes the
  // address one this browser has never been served a page at, so the answer comes
  // from the router and never from a cached copy of this page; it is a replace()
  // so the back button does not return to a page whose desktop is gone. Nothing on
  // that address can mint: an unknown session is answered with the ended page.
  var endedGoing = false;
  function ended(){
    if (endedGoing) return;
    endedGoing = true;
    try { console.log('[hdw4s gate] this desktop has ended: the router answered '
                      + '410 for its own session; showing the ended page'); } catch(e){}
    try { location.replace(location.pathname + '?hdw4s_ended=1'); } catch(e){}
  }

  // The desktop's published identity, read from the tree that is serving THIS page
  // rather than from anything the tab remembers about the world. A session start
  // republishes it, so two desktops behind one address never share a value.
  // Every failure -- not served, not readable, empty -- answers null, and null is a
  // refusal everywhere it is used. A resume that cannot establish which desktop it is
  // resuming must not happen: that is the logged-out row collapsing into the resume
  // row, which is the one outcome the arrival design exists to prevent.
  //
  // ONE ANSWER IS NOT A REFUSAL BUT A VERDICT: 410. The pool's router answers every
  // path under a session it no longer holds with 410 and its ended page, so a 410
  // here means THIS page's desktop is gone -- not that the file is missing. Measured
  // on production 2026-09-27, after a restart of the router: this page read the 410
  // as "no identity published", showed the card, and on the click loaded a client
  // script that was also answered 410, so the starting veil said "Your desktop is
  // starting" for ever. So a 410 leaves for the ended page at once. A named
  // desktop's door is the streaming server's own, which answers a missing file 404,
  // so this changes nothing there.
  function fetchIncarnation(cb){
    var done = false;
    function once(v){ if (!done) { done = true; cb(v); } }
    // A hidden tab's timers are throttled but its network is not; the timeout is
    // here so a hung request cannot leave the person looking at a blank page.
    setTimeout(function(){ once(null); }, 5000);
    try {
      fetch('hdw4s-incarnation', {cache: 'no-store'}).then(function(r){
        if (r.status === 410) { ended(); return once(null); }
        if (!r.ok) return once(null);
        return r.text().then(function(t){ once((t || '').trim() || null); },
                             function(){ once(null); });
      }, function(){ once(null); });
    } catch(e) { once(null); }
  }

  function show(k, label){
    body.innerHTML = k;
    go.textContent = label;
    if (BADKNOB.length)
      document.getElementById('hdw4s-err').textContent = 'ignored: ' + BADKNOB.join('; ');
    gate.hidden = false;
    // Whenever the card is on the screen the starting veil is not the thing to be
    // showing, and its poller has nothing left to wait for. The card draws above it
    // anyway, so this is about not leaving a timer running behind a state it does not
    // belong to -- which is how the stall backstop came to count a paused stream.
    veilDown();
  }

  // Second monitors and shared viewers are NEVER offered a takeover. They are not the
  // primary display, so being closed by the server does not mean somebody took the
  // desktop -- on this deployment #player2 is refused outright
  // (--enable-player2=false|locked) and the server KILLs it, which a QA run caught
  // being rendered as "the desktop was opened somewhere else" with a takeover button.
  var SECONDARY = /^#(shared|player[2-4]|display2)/.test(location.hash);

  function quietBanner(){
    // Upstream writes a terminated message and never clears it -- its overlay hides only
    // on a FIRST painted frame, and this page has had one. Left alone it would sit over
    // a live desktop after we reconnect. This is a TERMINAL-state substitution, the one
    // case where covering upstream's status is safe: v2 hid this banner on a timer and
    // raced its own detector, which is why detection now comes from the transport
    // instead.
    //
    // CORRECTION, and it is the sentence that hid a bug for a week: this comment used
    // to end "and nothing we detect depends on it." That stopped being true when
    // checkStatus() was added below, which detects on exactly this element -- and
    // because what follows only adds a CLASS, the text it reads is left behind for the
    // poller to find again. clearStaleStatus() is the other half; see boot().
    var s = document.getElementById('status-display');
    if (s && /terminated|superseded|disconnected/i.test(s.textContent||''))
      s.classList.add('hidden');
  }

  // The previous loss's residue, cleared before the card comes down.
  //
  // Measured, end to end: the recovery button works. selkiesCoreInitialize() is
  // present, the branch runs, the stream returns -- 179 decoded chunks in six seconds.
  // What fails is what the person sees. quietBanner() adds a class; checkStatus()
  // reads textContent, which a class does not touch, and upstream never clears that
  // text on a successful reconnect. So the poller, twice a second, reads the old
  // "Connection Terminated" the instant boot() sets gate.hidden = true -- at which
  // point lost() no longer no-ops, because the card is down -- and puts the card
  // straight back over a desktop that is streaming perfectly.
  //
  // CLEARED, not ignored, and the difference is the whole safety of it. A flag saying
  // "disregard the status for a while" would also disregard a REAL second loss
  // arriving inside that window, which is the defect this file exists to prevent. An
  // empty element cannot be read as a terminal state, and the next real terminal
  // message writes into it and is detected exactly as before.
  //
  // The class quietBanner() added is deliberately left on. Our card is what tells a
  // person their session ended; re-showing upstream's banner is a second change with
  // its own reasons, and doing two things here would make the test's verdict say
  // nothing about which one worked.
  function clearStaleStatus(){
    var s = document.getElementById('status-display');
    if (s) s.textContent = '';
  }
  function lost(why){
    if (SECONDARY) { window.__hdw4sLost = why; return; }   // recorded, never rendered
    if (!booted || !gate.hidden) return;
    window.__hdw4sLost = why; quietBanner(); show(LOST, LOST_GO);
  }

  // --- deterministic detection, from the transport, not from upstream's UI ----------
  // websockets: a "KILL " text frame. webrtc: the signalling socket closes 4001.
  var NativeWS = window.WebSocket;
  function WrappedWS(url, protocols){
    var ws = (protocols === undefined) ? new NativeWS(url) : new NativeWS(url, protocols);
    ws.addEventListener('message', function(ev){
      if (typeof ev.data === 'string' && ev.data.lastIndexOf('KILL ', 0) === 0) lost('kill');
    });
    ws.addEventListener('close', function(ev){ if (ev.code === 4001) lost('close4001'); });
    return ws;
  }
  WrappedWS.prototype = NativeWS.prototype;
  ['CONNECTING','OPEN','CLOSING','CLOSED'].forEach(function(k){ WrappedWS[k] = NativeWS[k]; });
  window.WebSocket = WrappedWS;

  // The WebRTC core announces an eviction with a NATIVE alert(), which blocks the main
  // thread until somebody clicks OK -- so nothing of ours can draw while it is up, and
  // to a person the tab looks frozen. Supplying our own alert() is providing a platform
  // function, not suppressing upstream's UI, and it turns that modal into our card.
  window.alert = function(msg){
    window.__hdw4sAlert = String(msg);
    if (/superseded|disconnected/i.test(String(msg))) lost('alert');
    else { try { console.warn('hdw4s: suppressed alert:', msg); } catch(e){} }
  };

  // The socket is in a WORKER by default (socket_worker=true), where the WebSocket
  // patch above cannot see the KILL frame -- measured: the card came back in ~15 s via
  // the backstop instead of ~3 s. Upstream writes its terminated text into
  // #status-display on every transport, so observing that element is the one signal
  // available in both configurations. Note this does NOT race the banner-hiding any
  // more: quietBanner() is called only from lost(), after the decision, never on a
  // timer -- which is what made v2 miss a takeover.
  //
  // Polled as well as observed, and the polling is the part that matters. A
  // MutationObserver only fires on a CHANGE, and upstream does not clear this text
  // on a successful reconnect: after a tab takes the session back, #status-display
  // still reads "Connection Terminated: ...". So when that same tab loses the
  // session a SECOND time, the text it would be set to is the text already there --
  // no mutation, no callback, and the card came back only via the ~10 s stall
  // backstop. Measured across two losses of one tab: first lost=status, second
  // lost=stalled, sameElement=True both times, so this is not a detached observer.
  // It is a signal that only exists on the edge, used to detect a level.
  var statusEl = null;
  function checkStatus(){
    if (!statusEl) return;
    var t = statusEl.textContent || '';
    if (/terminated|superseded|opened elsewhere/i.test(t)) lost('status');
  }
  function watchStatus(){
    statusEl = document.getElementById('status-display');
    if (!statusEl) return setTimeout(watchStatus, 500);
    new MutationObserver(checkStatus).observe(statusEl,
      {childList:true, characterData:true, subtree:true, attributes:true});
    checkStatus();
  }
  watchStatus();

  // Backstop, for a transport that announces nothing. THREE guards now, and each one
  // is a case where "no frames" is CORRECT rather than a disconnection:
  //   - never on a secondary viewer. #shared, #player2-4 and #display2 legitimately
  //     receive no video of their own, so "no frames" is their normal state, and the
  //     backstop was showing them a takeover button they must never see;
  //   - never before the stream has moved at least once, so a slow first frame is not
  //     read as a disconnection;
  //   - NEVER WHILE THE CLIENT HAS ASKED FOR THE STREAM TO STOP. See below.
  //
  // THE THIRD GUARD, and the defect it closes, reported by the owner from a live
  // desktop with his own console log as the evidence: the card appeared ON THE LIVE
  // PAGE WITH NO RELOAD, and his log reads "Tab hidden: Sent STOP_VIDEO". The frames
  // stopped because the client stopped them on purpose. The first two guards are
  // about a viewer that never receives video and about video that has not started
  // yet; nobody had guarded the state where video has started, is working, and has
  // been DELIBERATELY PAUSED. That state looks identical to a dead transport from a
  // frame counter alone, which is why the repair is not a longer timeout: a longer
  // timeout makes the wrong answer arrive later.
  //
  // ASK THE CLIENT, DO NOT TIME IT. The client knows -- it tracks
  // isVideoPipelineActive and logs "Received VIDEO_STOPPED" -- so there is a signal
  // to consult. It is read through a whitelist of places it has been seen, and a
  // FALSE reading is only trusted when it is explicit: if nothing here answers, the
  // fall-back is document.hidden, which is the CAUSE the client acts on and is
  // always available. An absent flag must not read as "the pipeline is running",
  // which is the shape that would put this defect straight back.
  function streamPaused(){
    var c = window.selkiesCore || window.selkies || window;
    if (typeof c.isVideoPipelineActive === 'boolean')
      return !c.isVideoPipelineActive;
    if (typeof window.isVideoPipelineActive === 'boolean')
      return !window.isVideoPipelineActive;
    return document.hidden;
  }

  var last = -1, still = 0, everMoved = false;
  // Reset on the way back as well as on the way out. A tab that has just been shown
  // again has a pipeline that is restarting, so the first tick or two after it
  // legitimately see no new frames -- and counting those is the same mistake one
  // step later.
  document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', function(){ still = 0; last = -1; });
  setInterval(function(){
    if (SECONDARY || !booted || !gate.hidden) { still = 0; return; }
    if (streamPaused()) { still = 0; last = -1; return; }
    // Cheap, and it is what makes a repeat loss show the card at once rather than
    // ten seconds later: lost() no-ops once the card is up, so this costs one
    // regexp against a short string twice a second.
    checkStatus();
    var n = window.videoChunksReceived || 0;
    if (n > 0) everMoved = true;
    still = (n === last) ? still + 1 : 0; last = n;
    if (everMoved && still >= 20) lost('stalled');   // ~10 s with no frames at all
  }, 500);

  // --- what a page is, said on the page --------------------------------------------
  // Measured, and every line below is a measurement rather than an intention:
  //   #shared    cannot type or point -- refused by the SERVER's input authority, not
  //              by its own page -- and RECEIVES the desktop's clipboard.
  //   #display2  full pointer, keyboard and clipboard both ways, with no card.
  //   both       are invisible to the primary, and outlive both the primary's tab and
  //              the desktop being replaced. There is no revocation.
  // Today a viewer is told none of this: those fragments boot ungated and the page
  // looks like any other. The badge is the half of "show an overlay when a session is
  // shared" that can honestly be done in the client. The other half -- telling the
  // PRIMARY somebody is there -- is measured NOT available to a page, and the panel
  // below says so instead of pretending.
  var ROLES = {
    shared:  '<b>You are watching someone’s desktop.</b> You cannot type or move '
           + 'the pointer. What they copy is shared with you. They are not told you '
           + 'are here.',
    display2:'<b>You have full control of someone’s desktop.</b> Keyboard, pointer '
           + 'and clipboard, both ways. They are not told you are here.',
    other:   '<b>You are a second display for someone’s desktop.</b>'
  };
  function roleOf(){
    var m = /^#(shared|display2|player[2-4])/.exec(location.hash);
    if (!m) return null;
    return ROLES[m[1]] || ROLES.other;
  }
  function notice(html){
    var el = document.getElementById('hdw4s-role');
    el.innerHTML = html; el.hidden = false;
  }

  var PANELS = {
    view: {
      frag: '#shared',
      html: '<p>Anyone with this link can <b>watch</b> this desktop.</p>'
          + '<p>They cannot type or move the pointer: the server refuses their input, '
          + 'not just their page.</p>'
          + '<p class="warn">They <b>do</b> receive this desktop’s clipboard. '
          + 'Everything you copy goes to them.</p>'
    },
    control: {
      frag: '#display2',
      html: '<p>Anyone with this link gets the <b>keyboard, pointer and clipboard</b> '
          + 'of this desktop, both ways.</p>'
          + '<p class="warn">They arrive with no announcement and no card.</p>'
    }
  };
  // Said on both panels, because it is the thing he is actually being asked to rule
  // on and it is the thing a screenshot of a working share would hide.
  var PANEL_TAIL =
      '<p class="blind">This tab cannot tell you whether anyone is connected, or who. '
    + 'That is measured, not an omission.</p>'
    + '<p class="blind">The link keeps working after you close this tab and after this '
    + 'desktop is replaced. Nothing takes it back.</p>'
    + '<p>Copying puts the link on your own clipboard — which this desktop also '
    + 'syncs, so the link can reach anyone already watching. Read it here instead if '
    + 'that matters.</p>';

  function mountShare(){
    if (SHARE === 'off' || roleOf()) return;   // never offered to a viewer
    var wrap  = document.getElementById('hdw4s-share');
    var panel = document.getElementById('hdw4s-panel');
    var btn   = document.getElementById('hdw4s-share-btn');
    var spec  = PANELS[SHARE];
    var url   = location.origin + location.pathname + location.search + spec.frag;
    panel.innerHTML = spec.html
      + '<input class="lnk" readonly value="' + url.replace(/"/g,'&quot;') + '">'
      + PANEL_TAIL
      + '<button type="button" id="hdw4s-copy">Copy link</button>';
    panel.querySelector('#hdw4s-copy').addEventListener('click', function(){
      var i = panel.querySelector('.lnk');
      i.select();
      var ok = false;
      try { ok = document.execCommand('copy'); } catch(e){}
      if (!ok && navigator.clipboard) navigator.clipboard.writeText(url).catch(function(){});
      this.textContent = 'Copied';
    });
    btn.addEventListener('click', function(){ panel.hidden = !panel.hidden; });
    wrap.hidden = false;
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // WHAT THE VISITOR IS LOOKING AT WHILE THE DESKTOP COMES UP
  //
  // THE DEFECT, and it is the owner's report: "frequently, i just get a black
  // streaming desktop. if i discard and retry, it eventually works. meanwhile, other
  // sessions continue working fine. so, this is only a problem at startup".
  //
  // MEASURED 2026-09-26 on a development box, through the real proxy hostname in a real browser
  // (private/measure-time-to-paint.py, four runs including three concurrent mints):
  // EVERY run showed the streamed picture at ONE distinct colour at the moment of
  // hand-over, and every one climbed to 2114-3161 colours nine to eleven seconds
  // later WITH NOTHING DISCARDED OR RESTARTED. Server-side, the same thing from the
  // other end: a slot whose X root read one colour was painted twenty minutes later
  // having never been touched, and on the black ones gnome-shell's own stage window
  // was absent from the root's child list while both painted controls had it
  // (private/measure-root-colours.sh).
  //
  // SO THE STREAM IS NOT BROKEN AND THE SLOT IS NOT POISONED: the desktop genuinely
  // has nothing on it yet. The session unit reports READY as soon as the streaming
  // server binds its port -- about a second into a start whose desktop takes another
  // nine, or on a loaded box a great deal longer -- and hdw4s-wait asks the same
  // question. Both are honest about what they check and neither is a claim that
  // anything has been painted. A LABEL IS NOT EVIDENCE, and the word doing the work
  // in "ready" was never "painted".
  //
  // WHAT THIS DOES AND, JUST AS IMPORTANTLY, DOES NOT DO. It does not delay the
  // connection: connecting early is what makes the stream exist at all, and holding
  // it back would make the wait longer rather than shorter. It delays only the
  // REVEAL, and it says what is happening while it does. Black with no explanation
  // reads as a fault; a sentence reads as a wait.
  //
  // IT FAILS OPEN, THREE WAYS, and that is the part not to simplify away:
  //
  //  1. IF IT CANNOT SAMPLE, IT COMES DOWN AT ONCE. No element to read, a context
  //     that will not give up pixels, an exception of any kind -- the veil goes. A
  //     veil that cannot measure must never hold a visitor behind itself, and every
  //     path out of here that is not "painted" is still a path out.
  //  2. IT GIVES UP ON A CLOCK. [[black-screen-diagnosis]] records a wedged
  //     compositor that ONLY A RESTART RECOVERS. That desktop is black forever, and
  //     a veil that waited for it would replace a visible defect with a permanent
  //     reassuring message -- strictly worse, because nobody would report it.
  //  3. IT DOES NOT COUNT WHILE THE STREAM IS STOPPED. Hiding the tab sends
  //     STOP_VIDEO and no frames arrive; counting that quiet as "still black" is the
  //     same mistake the stall backstop below already had to be repaired for.
  //
  // THE ORACLE IS THE PIXELS THE VISITOR IS BEING SENT, not the client's account of
  // itself. Frame counters were climbing throughout the black period in every run
  // above -- frames arriving proves the transport and never the picture.
  var VEIL_FLOOR = 16;      // distinct colours. A painted desktop measures thousands;
                            // one colour is black. Nothing needs to be close.
  // HOW LONG NOTHING AT ALL HAPPENS, and it is the owner's request in one number:
  // *"it makes the product feel slow, if it comes up, stays for 1 or 2 seconds and
  // then disappears ... the solution is to simply suppress the interstitial for a
  // short while."* And, once the startup hold made the desktop come up before the
  // page does: *"For the first couple of seconds, that doesn't warrant us to do
  // anything ... if it takes a lot longer than a few seconds, the user will wonder if
  // their button click even had any effect."* So anything resolving inside this
  // window shows no card at all.
  //
  // IT SUPPRESSES THE CARD AND NOTHING ELSE. The connection is made and the pixels
  // are sampled from the first tick as before -- delaying either would make the
  // wait longer rather than shorter, which is the opposite of the request. The only
  // thing on this timer is when the card becomes DUE; see armCard() for why due is
  // not the same as shown.
  var VEIL_SUPPRESS = 2000;
  // WHEN THE CARD STOPS PROMISING. It does NOT reveal here any more, and that
  // change is the repair for a defect that shipped this morning: this used to be
  // 90000, and the measured healthy cold start on the production box is 89.0
  // seconds against 15.0-15.3 on the development one (private/paint-evidence/).
  // A ONE-SECOND MARGIN between a healthy start and the fail-open, on the slower of
  // the two machines -- so a perfectly well session would have had its card torn
  // away to reveal black one second before the desktop arrived, and 90000 was
  // chosen when 15s was the only number anybody had.
  //
  // THE NUMBER IS NOT THE FIX. A second constant would rot exactly as the first
  // did, because it describes the machine and is written in the client. Two things
  // replace it:
  //
  //  * the card KEEPS RENDERING and changes what it SAYS, so being wrong about the
  //    number costs a visitor a sentence rather than a black screen. That decouples
  //    the threshold from the damage, which is what made 89-against-90 dangerous.
  //  * the threshold is LEARNED FROM THIS BOX. Every start that paints records how
  //    long it took, in this origin's own localStorage, and the patience is three
  //    times the slowest of the recent ones. A browser with no history gets the
  //    floor. So a box that is genuinely slow teaches the card to wait, rather than
  //    somebody having to notice and edit a number.
  //
  // THE FLOOR IS DERIVED TOO, from the one deadline the server already commits to:
  // hdw4s-proxy@.service carries TimeoutStartSec=180, so the front door itself
  // gives up at three minutes and there is nothing for this card to wait for past
  // that. Matching it is the honest ceiling; anything longer promises on behalf of
  // a component that has already stopped.
  var VEIL_PATIENCE_FLOOR = 180000;
  var PAINTS = 'hdw4s_paint_ms';   // this ORIGIN's, not this session's: the number
                                   // describes the box, and a key with the session
                                   // id in it would start empty on every arrival.
  var veilEl = document.getElementById('hdw4s-starting');
  var veilNote = document.getElementById('hdw4s-starting-note');
  var veilTitle = document.getElementById('hdw4s-starting-title');
  var veilAct = document.getElementById('hdw4s-starting-act');
  var veilTimer = null;
  var veilShowTimer = null;

  function recentPaints(){
    try {
      var v = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(PAINTS) || '[]');
      return Object.prototype.toString.call(v) === '[object Array]' ? v : [];
    } catch(e) { return []; }
  }

  function recordPaint(ms){
    // BEST EFFORT AND SILENT. A browser with storage blocked, a private window, a
    // quota that is full -- every one of those must cost nothing, because the only
    // thing lost is that this box teaches the card more slowly. The floor still
    // holds in every case.
    try {
      var a = recentPaints();
      a.push(Math.round(ms));
      while (a.length > 5) a.shift();
      localStorage.setItem(PAINTS, JSON.stringify(a));
    } catch(e){}
  }

  function veilPatience(){
    var a = recentPaints(), worst = 0;
    for (var i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
      var n = +a[i];
      // A stored value that is not a sane number is ignored rather than trusted:
      // this comes out of storage a page in another tab can also write.
      if (isFinite(n) && n > 0 && n < 3600000 && n > worst) worst = n;
    }
    return Math.max(VEIL_PATIENCE_FLOOR, worst * 3);
  }

  function veilDown(note){
    if (veilShowTimer) { clearTimeout(veilShowTimer); veilShowTimer = null; }
    if (veilTimer) { clearInterval(veilTimer); veilTimer = null; }
    if (veilEl) {
      // The class goes with the hide, so a card that is revealed again later
      // animates again instead of appearing already-finished. `hidden` is
      // display:none !important, so removing it mid-animation is instant -- which
      // is the "cancel the fade rather than complete it" case, and it needs no
      // special handling because there is nothing to wait for.
      veilEl.classList.remove('fade');
      veilEl.hidden = true;
    }
    if (note) { try { console.log('[hdw4s gate] ' + note); } catch(e){} }
  }

  // Count distinct colours in what the client has drawn. THREE outcomes, and keeping
  // them apart is the whole correctness of this thing:
  //
  //   a number   what is on the screen
  //   'waiting'  the client has not created its surface yet -- NOT a failure, and the
  //              caller must keep waiting. This case is why the function does not just
  //              return null: the first version collapsed "no canvas yet" and "cannot
  //              read this canvas" into one null, and since the client makes its canvas
  //              a moment AFTER the module loads, the fail-open fired on the very first
  //              tick of every single start. Measured: the veil flashed for under a
  //              second and the visitor got the black screen anyway, in five runs out
  //              of five, while every assertion about it passed. That is
  //              [[a-label-is-not-evidence]]'s one-bit-for-two-errnos, met again in
  //              our own repair for it.
  //   null       a surface exists and will not give up its pixels. THAT is the
  //              cannot-look case, and the caller reveals on it.
  //
  // Sampled through our own small canvas so a WebGL or bitmap-renderer client, whose
  // own context refuses getImageData, is still readable.
  function paintedColours(){
    try {
      var src = document.querySelector('canvas#videoCanvas, canvas, video');
      if (!src) return 'waiting';
      var w = src.videoWidth || src.width, h = src.videoHeight || src.height;
      if (!w || !h) return 'waiting';
      var c = document.createElement('canvas');
      c.width = 24; c.height = 24;
      var g = c.getContext('2d', {willReadFrequently: true});
      if (!g) return null;
      g.drawImage(src, 0, 0, 24, 24);
      var d = g.getImageData(0, 0, 24, 24).data, seen = {}, n = 0;
      for (var i = 0; i < d.length; i += 4) {
        var k = d[i] + ',' + d[i+1] + ',' + d[i+2];
        if (!seen[k]) { seen[k] = 1; n++; }
      }
      return n;
    } catch(e) { return null; }
  }

  function veilUp(){
    if (!veilEl) return;
    var t0 = Date.now();
    var patience = veilPatience();
    var gaveUp = false;
    // THE CARD IS SCHEDULED; THE SAMPLING IS NOT. Everything that could end the
    // wait starts now, and only the card's appearance is on a timer.
    //
    // AND THE TIMER ONLY MAKES THE CARD DUE. It is SHOWN by tick(), in the same step
    // as a sample that has just read "not painted" -- never on the timer's own say.
    // Measured 2026-09-27 on the production box, five fresh arrivals in a real
    // Chrome: the first frame landed 520-560 ms after this function ran, just AFTER
    // the 500 ms tick, so the timer put the card up at 800 over a desktop that was
    // already on the screen and the 1000 ms tick took it down again -- four arrivals
    // out of five (private/evidence/veil-flash-20260927/). A longer window alone moves
    // that race rather than closing it: a frame landing between the last tick and the
    // deadline does it again, and ticks were measured running late. Showing only
    // from the sampling step closes it at any timing. Three earlier repairs each
    // fixed a real defect nearby and missed this one; keep the property, not the
    // numbers.
    var cardDue = false;
    function armCard(){
      if (veilShowTimer) clearTimeout(veilShowTimer);
      cardDue = false;
      veilShowTimer = setTimeout(function(){
        veilShowTimer = null;
        cardDue = true;
        tick();
      }, VEIL_SUPPRESS);
    }
    armCard();

    // A LATCH, BECAUSE THE FIRST SAMPLE HAPPENS BEFORE THE POLLER EXISTS. Without
    // it, an immediate paint called veilDown() -- which clears a timer that had not
    // been created yet -- and the line at the bottom then started a poller for a
    // card that was already down, leaving a 500 ms interval running for the life of
    // the page. Read at the bottom rather than inferred from veilTimer.
    var stopped = false;
    function stop(note){ stopped = true; veilDown(note); }

    function tick(){
      // (3) above: the question comes BEFORE the reading, never after.
      //
      // AND WHILE WE CANNOT LOOK, THE CARD MUST NOT ARRIVE EITHER. Returning
      // here left the suppression timer running, so being unable to sample
      // GUARANTEED the card -- and the case where we cannot sample is exactly
      // the case where the desktop is already up. Reported 2026-09-26,
      // reconnecting to a session the browser had stopped: *"i see the screen
      // pop up with life content, then it briefly fades black, shows the pop up
      // for too short of a time to read, and then it disappears and i again see
      // the life desktop"*. A stopped stream is a paused pipeline, so the
      // immediate sample below -- whose whole purpose is that an already
      // painted desktop costs no card -- was skipped in the one situation it
      // was written for. The card then faded in over a live picture and was
      // pulled straight back off when the pipeline resumed.
      //
      // Pushing the window out rather than cancelling it: if the stream never
      // resumes, nobody is looking at this tab, and the patience branch below
      // is likewise unreachable while paused. Both stay quiet together.
      if (document.visibilityState === 'hidden' || streamPaused()) { armCard(); return; }
      var n = paintedColours();
      if (n === 'waiting') { /* no surface yet: the patience below still bounds us */ }
      else if (n === null) { stop('cannot sample the picture; revealing'); return; }
